# PerfPilot Project and Coding Guidelines

## Scope and conflicting instructions

- Apply these rules to all PerfPilot components, including the Agent Framework,
  A2A server, AG-UI server, MCP servers, frontend, and deployment files.
- Companion rules supplement these requirements; they do not grant permission
  to bypass approvals, run restricted commands, install software, or refactor.
- If repository instructions conflict, explain the conflict and ask the user
  before proceeding. Do not silently choose a less restrictive interpretation.

## Rule precedence and engineering references

- These global governance, security, execution, and approval restrictions apply
  before component-specific guidance. Companion rules and engineering documents
  supplement these restrictions but never grant permission to bypass them.
- For Python work (including planning, refactoring, and testing), consult
  `.cursor/rules/implementation-standards.mdc` and the relevant sections of
  `docs/engineering/development-workflow.md`.
- For technology-specific work, consult
  `.cursor/rules/technology-reference-policy.mdc` and
  `docs/engineering/technology-stack.md`.
- Public engineering documents supply shared context and conventions; they do
  not independently authorize commands or expand an approved change scope.
- When applicable instructions conflict, identify the conflict and ask the
  user before proceeding. Do not silently relax a restriction.

## Implementation principles

1. All Git commands require explicit user approval, including read-only commands.
   Never push or pull. Do not commit, merge, create branches, or submit PRs.
2. Run commands only from and within the repository root and its descendants.
   Ask the user when an operation requires access outside this boundary.
3. Do not install software or dependencies. Ask the user to install them manually.
4. Do not refactor existing code, change existing function signatures, or
   restructure modules unless explicitly approved. Keep approved changes scoped;
   do not use a coding guideline as permission for unrelated cleanup.
5. Follow existing async patterns, type hints, docstrings, and component style.
6. Search for existing modules and equivalent behavior before adding code.
   Create new files only when justified by a clear responsibility or enhancement.
7. Preserve compatibility with existing workflows. Report a proposed breaking
   change and obtain approval before implementing it.
8. Redact private user, company, corporate, endpoint, hostname, and service data
   from shared material. Ask when uncertain whether a value is private.
9. Validate each enhancement before moving to the next. Run only approved tests
   and report what was and was not verified.

## Feature-branch requirement

- Modify files only on a user-confirmed feature branch, never main.
- Establish the branch through an explicitly approved read-only Git command or
  ask the user to confirm the current branch before editing.
- If the branch is main, unknown, detached, or otherwise unsuitable, stop before
  editing and ask the user to create or switch to the intended feature branch.
- This requirement does not authorize any Git command or branch operation.

## Planning and approval

- For non-trivial work, use Plan Mode or create a self-contained implementation
  plan and task list in .cursor/docs/plans/ relative to the repository root.
- Keep internal plans in that gitignored location; never publish them as public
  documentation. If its ignored status is uncertain, ask before writing sensitive
  planning material. Do not run Git to check without approval.
- Ask clarifying questions, identify affected components and relevant references,
  describe proposed files and tests, and state assumptions and scope exclusions.
- Present the plan for review and obtain final approval before code changes.
- Revise the plan when feedback or new evidence materially changes the work.
- Do not expand a rules/documentation task into implementation fixes without
  separate approval. Record discovered implementation gaps for follow-up.

## Perplexity MCP: ask before guessing

- Perplexity MCP is optional for contributors; it is not required to develop or
  contribute to PerfPilot. When configured, prefer it for technical research.
- When uncertain about an API, specification, current documentation, or approach,
  research using available tools and official sources rather than inventing an answer.
- If Perplexity MCP is configured, use perplexity_search for source discovery,
  perplexity_ask for focused questions, perplexity_research for deeper multi-source
  investigation, and perplexity_reason for architecture or design tradeoffs.
- Tool names and availability may differ. Use only tools actually exposed by the
  configured integration. Without Perplexity, use other available research tools
  or official documentation. If essential information cannot be verified, disclose
  the limitation and ask for the source or guidance; never pretend verification occurred.
- Verify consequential technical details against the underlying official source.
- Briefly disclose research-tool usage and record useful source URLs in the plan
  or engineering reference material. Never send secrets or private repository
  content to an external research service without explicit authorization.

## Security and secrets

- Never write, print, log, or expose real credentials, tokens, passwords, keys,
  connection strings, or other secrets in code, comments, plans, or shared output.
- If a secret appears exposed or present in a tracked/public-facing file, stop
  and notify the user without reproducing its value. Request remediation before
  continuing affected work. Gitignore alone does not establish secret safety.
- Prefer environment-variable or secrets-manager references for configuration.
- Redact secret-like values in logs, examples, and diffs even when they appear
  to be placeholders. Do not remove or rotate credentials without authorization.
- Keep secret-handling instructions portable across Windows and macOS. Do not
  assume shell syntax, filesystem paths, or credential-store locations are the
  same; follow the contributor's operating system and active deployment workflow.
- For local Docker Compose workflows, environment variables and untracked `.env`
  files may supply configuration. Treat `.env` values as sensitive, never commit
  populated files, and do not mistake them for a production secrets manager.
- For local Aspire workflows, follow the project's .NET `user-secrets` setup
  instead of assuming Compose `.env` conventions. User secrets are intended for
  local development, not production secret storage.
- For deployed environments, use the approved secret provider or deployment
  configuration (for example, an explicitly configured vault); never assume
  local development secret stores are available in containers or the cloud.
- Provide sanitized examples and platform-appropriate setup guidance rather
  than imposing the maintainer's personal machine configuration on contributors.

## Database access and data safety

- Treat all persistent databases and their data as protected resources, whether
  accessed locally or remotely, through `psql`, a VS Code extension, a Python
  script, a migration framework, Docker/Aspire commands, or an MCP tool.
- Coding agents must **not execute database-changing operations**. This includes
  SQL DML/DDL writes, schema or data migrations, seed/reset scripts, TRUNCATE,
  DELETE, DROP, bulk imports, restore operations, and modification of persistent
  database files or volumes. The user/operator executes such operations.
- Agents may draft database commands, migration SQL, and rollback instructions
  for the user's review and manual execution. Clearly identify the target
  environment, intended effects, prerequisites, backup considerations, and
  irreversible risks. Never represent a draft as executed or verified.
- Do not execute live database queries, including read-only inspection, without
  explicit user approval for the target and purpose. Prefer reviewing repository
  schemas, models, migration files, and documented contracts first.
- Do not circumvent these restrictions by invoking commands indirectly through
  shell scripts, application startup, tests, IDE integrations, containers,
  CI jobs, or other tools. Inspect side effects before running such commands.
- This is an **agent-operation policy** for this repository, not a prohibition
  on the application's authorized runtime writes or on contributors operating
  their own environments. Do not modify application behavior to enforce it.
- If testing requires mutable database state, propose an isolated disposable
  test environment and obtain authorization for the setup and execution plan;
  do not autonomously run state-changing database operations.

## Tests and diagnostic scripts

- Maintained unit/regression tests belong under the relevant component's tests/
  directory and must be version-controlled for CI.
- Never place maintained CI tests in the gitignored scripts/ directory.
- Before creating a standalone verification script, consider a repeatable test.
- Unit tests cover isolated behavior; integration/contract tests cover service
  or protocol boundaries. Follow existing test organization and distinguish
  external/infrastructure-dependent checks from ordinary unit tests.
- Reserve scripts/ for approved temporary diagnostics or operator utilities.
- Do not install test tools or establish a frontend test framework without approval.
- Never run load tests or tests against external, production, or production-like
  targets without explicit authorization for the target and execution scope.

## Documentation and monthly changelog

- Review documentation affected by changes to behavior, configuration, APIs,
  environment variables, architecture, deployment, tests, or developer workflows.
- Update affected public documentation in the same change. If none is affected,
  state why in the completion summary. Describe implemented behavior accurately;
  distinguish planned capabilities from existing functionality.
- Before PR handoff, update the repository-root CHANGELOG.md using the existing
  entry format. Include relevant agent-rule and documentation changes.
- Monthly archives live in docs/changelogs/ and use CHANGELOG-YYYY-MM.md.
- Inspect the established rollover procedure before moving entries. If timing,
  date basis, or entry ownership is ambiguous, ask rather than invent a policy.
- Preserve history. Do not overwrite an existing archive or duplicate a month
  without reviewing the contents and obtaining guidance where needed.
- Changelog maintenance does not authorize Git operations or PR submission.

## Docstring and comment hygiene

- Write self-contained explanations of what the code does and why.
- Do not reference PBI numbers, Feature IDs, Bug/ticket IDs, ADO artifacts, or
  internal .cursor/docs/ planning filenames in source comments or docstrings.
- Keep internal work-item mappings in ADO or private plans, not public code.
- Ask: would a public contributor understand this without internal context?
  If not, rewrite it.

## Completion report

- Summarize changes, documentation/changelog updates, and verification results.
- Identify unresolved assumptions, blocked checks, and follow-up recommendations.
- Never claim tests passed, documentation was read, or compliance was verified
  unless those actions actually occurred.
